  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nKDDC radar observations show the decaying remnants of a marginally \nsevere thunderstorm complex are spread out over portions of \nsouthwest KS, with an outflow boundary racing southward across our \nsouthern zones. Convective activity will continue to taper off \nthrough the evening as the boundary layer stabilizes, and the \novernight period will be mostly quiet as temperatures drop into the \nmid-60s northwest to the upper 70s southeast. Daytime Friday, short \nrange ensembles agree a mid-level ridge will remain stationary over \nsouthwestern Dixie Alley, resulting in southwesterly 500-mb flow \nover the central plains. The previous night's rainfall/convective \noutflow will help shave a few degrees off afternoon temperatures for \nmost locations, with highs ranging from the low 90s northwest to 104-\n107 southeast. Another round of scattered, marginally severe \nthunderstorms is expected Friday afternoon as all HREF members show \nconvection developing in the vicinity of the retreating outflow \nboundary by 21-23Z. Similar to Thursday evening, the primary threat \nwith this activity will be isolated severe wind gusts, especially \nacross our northwest zones where CAMs depict the most pronounced \nclustering.\n\nDaytime Saturday, ensembles suggest little to no change in the \nsynoptic pattern as a mid-level ridge holds fast in the southeastern \nCONUS. This will engender a nearly identical forecast for Saturday \nas Friday, as afternoon highs in the mid 90s northwest to 103-106 \nsoutheast are followed by scattered, late afternoon/early evening, \nmarginally severe thunderstorms posing primary a severe wind gust \nrisk.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe upper level ridge will retrograde to the Desert Southwest by \nmidday Monday, and move very little through the end of next business \nweek. Multiple additional rounds of precipitation will reinforce the \nslightly cooler airmass across southwest KS, providing a much needed \nbreak from the intense heat wave as afternoon highs are in the mid \n80s to low 90s both Sunday and Monday. However, this will be short \nlived as the retrograding ridge suppresses precipitation chances and \nallows temperatures to rebound into the mid/upper 90s.",
